---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The Standard"
  description="The test every product has to pass before we sell it under this name — and the list of things that failed."
>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <span class="meta-strip">The Standard · v1.0 · Published August 2026</span>
      <h1>What has to be true before we'll sell you something.</h1>
      <p>
        This page exists so that our claims can be checked rather than believed. It is versioned and
        dated. When it changes, the old version stays up.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The five tests</h2>

      <ol class="tests" style="margin-bottom:40px;">
        <li>
          <b>It has to beat what we'd otherwise recommend.</b>
          If someone else already makes the best version of a thing, we will tell you to buy theirs
          and we will not make a competing one. Being able to make it is not a reason to make it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>It has to do less than the competition, not more.</b>
          If the only way to justify a product is a longer feature list, a bigger page count or more
          SKUs, it fails. Every product should be defensible on what it leaves out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The reasoning has to be publishable.</b>
          If we can't explain in public why this and not that, we don't ship it. Every product page
          carries the alternatives we considered and why they lost.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>We have to be able to name its flaws.</b>
          Every product page says who the product is <em>not</em> for. If we can't think of anyone it
          isn't for, we haven't understood it well enough to sell it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>We have to use it ourselves.</b>
          Not once, for a photograph. Regularly, in the way a customer would.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What this rules out</h2>
      <p>
        Deliberately, and permanently: manufactured urgency, countdown timers, inflated MRPs with a
        line through them, fake scarcity, undisclosed affiliate links, sponsored placement, paid
        reviews, and any claim we can't show the working for.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also rules out growth by catalogue. We expect the number of things we sell to stay
        embarrassingly small. That is a decision, not a stage we're passing through.
      </p>

      <h2>How we're funded</h2>
      <p>
        By people paying for things we made. Not by display advertising — we tried that, it didn't
        work, and it pointed our incentives at traffic instead of usefulness. There are no ad units
        on this site.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we ever use an affiliate link, it will be labelled as one in the same sentence, not in a
        footer disclosure.
      </p>

      <h2>Things that failed these tests</h2>
      <p>
        This list will be longer than the list of things we sell. That's the point of having a
        standard at all.
      </p>

      <ul class="failed-list">
        <li>
          <b>The Crux Monthly (e-magazine)</b>
          <span>Announced October 2026, never built. Failed test 1 — it was a format decision, not a thing anyone had asked for.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The Crux Markets (daily brief)</b>
          <span>Announced for early 2027, never built. Failed test 2 — it was the news product again, with a narrower audience and the same economics.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Branded merchandise</b>
          <span>Failed test 5. We would not have used it. A logo on a tote bag is not a product.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A cross-brand membership</b>
          <span>Failed test 3. We could not write a sentence explaining what problem it solved for anyone other than us.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If something on this site is wrong, or a product doesn't do what we said it does,
        <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. Corrections get published on the page where the mistake was,
        not quietly edited out.
      </p>

      <hr />
      <p class="meta-strip">
        Version 1.0 · August 2026 · Previous versions will be listed here as they're replaced.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
